1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:05,090
The brush clears a wheel carved with spokes, the wheel of dharma.

2
00:00:05,090 --> 00:00:09,873
It stands for a faith that a king, Harsha, supported with all his wealth.

3
00:00:09,873 --> 00:00:14,255
Every five years, he is said to have given away everything but his clothes.

4
00:00:14,255 --> 00:00:18,048
Why would a king give away everything every five years?

5
00:00:34,783 --> 00:00:40,057
How do we know? The Harshacharita was written by Bana, Harsha's court poet.

6
00:00:40,057 --> 00:00:45,094
Hiuen Tsang, a Chinese monk, travelled in India for fourteen years.

7
00:00:45,094 --> 00:00:51,358
And inscriptions, such as the Banskhera plate and the Aihole inscription, give more.

8
00:01:06,583 --> 00:01:14,964
Harshavardhana was born into the Pushyabhuti family of Thanesar. His brother Rajyavardhana was killed by Shashanka of Gauda.

9
00:01:14,964 --> 00:01:23,077
Harsha became king in 606 CE, at sixteen. He made Kannauj his capital, and united much of north India.

10
00:01:23,077 --> 00:01:31,131
He then moved against the south, but was defeated by the Chalukya king Pulakeshin the Second on the Narmada, about 618 CE.

11
00:01:31,131 --> 00:01:35,075
Hiuen Tsang visited his court, and stayed at Nalanda.

12
00:01:35,075 --> 00:01:42,909
He describes a great assembly at Kannauj, and a gathering at Prayag, held every five years, where Harsha gave away his wealth.

13
00:01:42,909 --> 00:01:50,696
Harsha was also a writer. He is credited with three plays: Ratnavali, Priyadarshika and Nagananda.

14
00:01:50,696 --> 00:01:58,170
His state was less centralised than the Mauryas, and relied on feudatories. Officers were often paid with land.

15
00:01:58,170 --> 00:02:03,762
Harsha died in 647 CE without an heir, and his empire broke up.

16
00:02:18,983 --> 00:02:25,806
Old name, new name. The Chinese pilgrim is now written Xuanzang in modern romanisation.

17
00:02:25,806 --> 00:02:29,773
Older books spell him Hiuen Tsang, or Hsuan-tsang.

18
00:02:29,773 --> 00:02:35,862
He walked to India, studied at Nalanda, and returned with hundreds of Buddhist texts.

19
00:02:51,083 --> 00:03:02,421
Carve this tip into memory. 606 to 647, Kannauj capital. Hiuen Tsang watched. Two dates, one capital and a witness.

20
00:03:02,421 --> 00:03:06,505
Add the defeat on the Narmada, and you have full marks.

21
00:03:21,717 --> 00:03:37,633
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Who was Harsha’s court poet and biographer? The answer: Bana. Number two.

22
00:03:37,633 --> 00:03:53,648
Which Chalukya king defeated Harsha? The answer: Pulakeshin the Second. Number three. Name the capital of Harsha.

23
00:03:53,648 --> 00:04:03,573
The answer: Kannauj. Now one line for Mains. Evaluate Harsha’s achievements and the accounts of Hiuen Tsang.

24
00:04:22,783 --> 00:04:30,344
The wheel goes into its case. It is a symbol of the teaching that Harsha supported. Three facts to take away.

25
00:04:30,344 --> 00:04:42,632
A wheel, the symbol of dharma. Harsha ruled from 606 to 647 CE. Hiuen Tsang visited his court. Next gallery: The Pallavas.

